<Project>

  <!--
    Analysis gate (issue #31). AD-8 already makes the AOT and trim analyzers a
    build-time gate rather than a release-time surprise; this does the same for
    the Roslyn correctness and style analyzers, and does it in one place so a
    new project cannot join the solution with a weaker setting than its
    siblings.

    `latest-all` enables every rule the SDK's analyzer package ships, including
    the ones off by default. Everything downgraded from that maximum lives in
    the repo-root .editorconfig, one line per rule with the reason it was
    downgraded — so the exceptions are a readable list rather than a property
    nobody re-reads. It is .editorconfig and not .globalconfig deliberately: a
    global AnalyzerConfig has no glob sections, so `[tests/**/*.cs]` is not
    expressible there, and it would lose to the per-rule config the SDK
    generates for `latest-all` anyway.

    `EnforceCodeStyleInBuild` is what makes the IDExxxx style rules reach a
    command-line build at all; without it .editorconfig is advice the IDE gives
    and CI never checks. `CodeAnalysisTreatWarningsAsErrors` is separate from
    `TreatWarningsAsErrors` and defaults to false, so analyzer findings would
    otherwise stay warnings while compiler findings failed the build.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest-all</AnalysisLevel>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <CodeAnalysisTreatWarningsAsErrors>true</CodeAnalysisTreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Version stamping (issue #10 / PRD CLI-17).

    The whole flow is the SDK's own, deliberately: this file adds a default and
    nothing else, so there is no bespoke version arithmetic to go wrong.

      dotnet publish -p:Version=1.0.0-rc.14 -p:SourceRevisionId=abc1234

    yields, via Microsoft.NET.GenerateAssemblyInfo.targets (verified empirically
    against the SDK in mise.toml, 10.0.400):

      AssemblyVersion            1.0.0.0            (numeric core; prerelease dropped)
      AssemblyFileVersion        1.0.0.0
      AssemblyInformationalVersion  1.0.0-rc.14+abc1234

    `okf version` prints the informational attribute, so both the release
    candidate number and the commit it was built from survive into the shipped
    binary. `-p:Version` must be a bare semver — the leading `v` of a git tag is
    stripped by whoever passes it (the `publish` CI job, the `publish-aot` mise
    task), never here.

    `SourceRevisionId` is optional. The SDK's built-in source-control support
    fills it in from the working tree when there is a `.git` to read, so a plain
    `dotnet build` already produces `0.0.0-dev+<40-char-sha>`; passing it
    explicitly overrides that with the short sha the release actually names.
    Neither this file nor any target in this repo shells out to git — deriving a
    version from the working tree is the caller's job, so a build from a source
    archive with no `.git` still works and still produces a usable version.

    ISSUE #53 — TAGGED RELEASES PRINT THE BARE VERSION
    ====================================================
    A build the `publish` CI job stamps from a tag already has an identifier for
    the commit — the tag itself, one `git show <tag>` away. Appending `+<sha>`
    to that build's `AssemblyInformationalVersion` bought nothing and cost a
    fresh install printing `okf 1.0.0+9765a305` — build metadata precedence
    already ignores (semver §10), on a version a release note already names
    exactly. `IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion` is the SDK's own
    switch for this: the `publish` job now passes
    `-p:IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion=false` so
    `AssemblyInformationalVersion` is the bare `-p:Version` and nothing more.
    `mise run publish-aot` and a plain `dotnet build` do not pass it, so a local
    or untagged build keeps `+<sha>` — nothing else says where an unstamped
    build came from.

    The commit must not disappear just because it left the informational
    version: every build still carries `SourceRevisionId` as its own attribute,
    independent of whether it was folded into the informational version
    string, so `okf version`'s verbose flag can print it as a second line. Read via
    `Assembly.GetCustomAttributes<AssemblyMetadataAttribute>()`, which is
    AOT-safe — no reflection over types, the same shape as the
    `AssemblyInformationalVersionAttribute` lookup `okf version` already does.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      The unstamped default. Left to the SDK this would be `1.0.0`, which is a
      lie a dev build tells with a straight face — and, once main flips to
      non-prerelease versioning, indistinguishable from the real 1.0.0. A build
      nobody stamped says so.
    -->
    <Version Condition="'$(Version)' == ''">0.0.0-dev</Version>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Carries the commit forward even when `IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion`
    drops it from `AssemblyInformationalVersion` (issue #53). Deliberately a Target, not a
    plain `ItemGroup`: a bare `<AssemblyMetadata Include="Commit" Value="$(SourceRevisionId)" />`
    outside any target bakes in `$(SourceRevisionId)` at project-evaluation time, before the
    SDK's `InitializeSourceControlInformation` target has resolved it from git — empirically
    confirmed empty (`commit: unknown` even on a build whose first line carried `+<sha>`
    fine, because that suffix is appended by a Target too). `BeforeTargets="GetAssemblyAttributes"`
    plus `DependsOnTargets="InitializeSourceControlInformation"` gives the item the same
    timing `AddSourceRevisionToInformationalVersion` already relies on for the `+<sha>`
    suffix. Empty when neither `-p:SourceRevisionId` nor git supplies one (a source archive
    with no `.git`, built with no override); `okf version`'s verbose flag prints that as
    `commit: unknown`.
  -->
  <Target Name="AddCommitAssemblyMetadata"
          BeforeTargets="GetAssemblyAttributes"
          DependsOnTargets="InitializeSourceControlInformation">
    <ItemGroup>
      <AssemblyMetadata Include="Commit" Value="$(SourceRevisionId)" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

</Project>
